eld-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "has infinitely many solutions"] ["text" "has no solution"] ["text" "has the unique solution $y≡0$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-339176" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "has infinitely many solutions"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0)+{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}(0)+{y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1)-{y}_{1}^{′}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)-{y}_{2}^{′}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$  {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Robin boundary conditions, given by\n\n$$\ny(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, setting ${\\alpha }_{1}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{1}=1,$ ${\\alpha }_{2}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{2}=-1,$ and $a=0,$  $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ becomes\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0)+{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}(0)+{y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1)-{y}_{1}^{′}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)-{y}_{2}^{′}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-3m+2 & =0 \\\\ (m-1)(m-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have ${m}_{1}=1$ and ${m}_{2}=2,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0}+{e}^{0} & {e}^{3(0)}+2{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{1}-{e}^{1} & {e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{2(1)}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =2(-{e}^{2})-0(3) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has a unique solution }y≡0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "379a162431a4384226549537fb95ccaf9a02797a5cb5c466b650b7158d7d88da"} {"question" "q-338980" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338980" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "det(A)=0"] ["math" "det(A)\\ne 0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338980" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "det(A)\\ne 0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338980" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/C