if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$  {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2m-3 & =0 \\\\ (m-3)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{3} & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={e}^{-1}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "379a162431a4384226549537fb95ccaf9a02797a5cb5c466b650b7158d7d88da"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "det(A)=0"] ["math" "det(A)\\ne 0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "det(A)\\ne 0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$"] ["text" "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$"] ["text" "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$"] ["text" "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1-{e}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{e}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{4}}{e}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{e}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "has infinitely many solutions"] ["text" "has no solution"] ["text" "has the unique solution $y≡0$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338525" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "has the unique solution $y≡0$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-339297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)-y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)-y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-339297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 1.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A{e}^{x}-B{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)-y(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B-(A+B) & =0 \\\\ -2B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(1)-y(1)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nA{e}^{1}-A{e}^{1}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is\n\n$$\ny=A\\cdot {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-339297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "379a162431a4384226549537fb95ccaf9a02797a5cb5c466b650b7158d7d88da"} {"question" "q-339297" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-339297" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e^x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-339475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-339475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm \\text{i}.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\\,⇒\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ where $A$ is arbitrary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-339475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "379a162431a4384226549537fb95ccaf9a02797a5cb5c466b650b7158d7d88da"} {"question" "q-339475" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-339475" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\cos(x)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,y(-2)=0,\\,y(2)-4{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,-2\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(-2)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2A+B=0\\,⇒\\,B=2A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=Ax+2A=A(x+2).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(2)-4{y}^{′}(2)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A-4A=0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot (x+2),$ where $A$ is arbitrary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "379a162431a4384226549537fb95ccaf9a02797a5cb5c466b650b7158d7d88da"} {"question" "q-338544" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338544" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,y(-4)=0,\\,y(0)-4{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,-4\\le x\\le 0\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(-4)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-4A+B=0\\,⇒\\,B=4A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=Ax+4A=A(x+4).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(0)-4{y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A-4A=0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot (x+4),$ where $A$ is arbitrary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "379a162431a4384226549537fb95ccaf9a02797a5cb5c466b650b7158d7d88da"} {"question" "q-338599" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338599" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)-4y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-4y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4.$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)-4y(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4A-4B)-4(A+B) & =0 \\\\ -8B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-4y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{2}-4A{e}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot {e}^{4x},$ where $A$ is arbitrary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "379a162431a4384226549537fb95ccaf9a02797a5cb5c466b650b7158d7d88da"} {"question" "q-338645" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "q-338645" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e^{4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/state.json" "file_sha256" "906452b5f61d8bd239d9985f5e577da74b5e75a810b28c1b351ff08cbc6c7193"} {"question" "e-21797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' - 3y = 0,  \\qquad y'(1) = y'(3) = 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le3\n$$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/assets/aa58b04d0ebdd18e4259fd3a1386332043f317f12b94c7b4ad468bef77584a0f.png)\n\nFind the fundamental solutions and use $A$ to determine if the given BVP has a unique or infinitely many solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/example-21797.json" "file_sha256" "ed5813bcd13359040a6bc2f304cec01a6f11087340737545281b377acb0fe9ca"} {"question" "e-21797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x)+P(x)y'(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_{1}y(a)+\\beta_{1}y'(a)=0,\\qquad\n\\alpha_{2}y(b)+\\beta_{2}y'(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i, \\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)\\ne(0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$  \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/assets/ac7be07071df654f4d2fec18b6b93afb21fd13169e7890ea091b496d7f9b206c.png)\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y\\equiv0$ if and only if $\\det(A)\\ne0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions.  So, setting $\\alpha_i=0,$ $\\beta_i=1,$ $a=1,$ and $b=3,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/assets/91f85685d5a17221398842923709c20b0e296e53fedb30ee6a4fdcb16d99e0d8.png)\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y=e^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2m-3 & =0 \\\\ (m-3)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_{1}(x)=e^{3x}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;y_{2}(x)=e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2'(x) = -e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3(1)} & -{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3(3)} & -{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3} & -{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{9} & -{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(3{e}^{3})(-{e}^{-3})-(-{e}^{-1})(3{e}^{9}) \\\\  & =-3+3{e}^{8} \\\\  & =3({e}^{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)\\ne0,$ we conclude that this BVP has the unique trivial solution $y\\equiv0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/example-21797.json" "file_sha256" "ed5813bcd13359040a6bc2f304cec01a6f11087340737545281b377acb0fe9ca"} {"question" "e-21799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\ny'' = 0, \\qquad y(4) = 0, \\quad y(3) + y'(3) = 0,\\quad 3\\leq x \\leq 4\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/example-21799.json" "file_sha256" "47960b1ecbe0c9074d8f6bcbb7de669434ccc5da82221a89219dc20982ff708a"} {"question" "e-21799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\ny''(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny' = A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(4) = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A + B = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad B = -4A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny = Ax - 4A = A(x-4).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(3) + y'(3) = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-A+A = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y = A\\cdot \\left(x-4\\right),$\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/example-21799.json" "file_sha256" "47960b1ecbe0c9074d8f6bcbb7de669434ccc5da82221a89219dc20982ff708a"}] "usage" {"e-21797" [] "e-21799" [] "q-338544" [] "q-338525" []} "basis" 2584 "retractions" [[17592186229667 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″} - 2y^{′} - 3y = 0, y(0) = y(1) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le 1$\n\nLet $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}(0) & y_{2}(0) \\\\ y_{1}(1) & y_{2}(1)\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\nThe fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\nFor this BVP, we have $det(A) =$ {{field-3}}.\n\nTherefore, this BVP {{field-4}}."] [17592186229667 :question/answer-fields 17592186228420] [17592186229670 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y^{″} = 0, y(-2) = 0, y(2) - 4y^{′}(2) = 0,-2 \\le x \\le 2$\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A$.\n\nThe solution is $y = A \\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)$."] [17592186228886 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0, \\qquad y'(1) = y'(3) = 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le3$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(1) & y_{2}^{′}(1) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(3) & y_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the fundamental solutions and use $A$ to determine if the given BVP has a unique or infinitely many solutions."] [17592186228886 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x)+P(x)y'(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_{1}y(a)+\\beta_{1}y'(a)=0,\\qquad \\alpha_{2}y(b)+\\beta_{2}y'(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, and $\\alpha_i, \\beta_i$ are real constants such that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)\\ne(0,0)\n$$\n\nFurthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$.\n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}α_{1}y_{1}(a) + β_{1}y_{1}^{′}(a) & α_{1}y_{2}(a) + β_{1}y_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ α_{2}y_{1}(b) + β_{2}y_{1}^{′}(b) & α_{2}y_{2}(b) + β_{2}y_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y\\equiv0$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\det(A)\\ne0\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting $\\alpha_i=0$, $\\beta_i=1$, $a=1$, and $b=3$, the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(1) & y_{2}^{′}(1) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(3) & y_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y=e^{mx}$. The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} - 2m - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(m - 3)(m + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1$, and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_{1}(x)=e^{3x}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;y_{2}(x)=e^{-x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2'(x) = -e^{-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{3(1)} & -e^{-1} \\\\ 3e^{3(3)} & -e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{3} & -e^{-1} \\\\ 3e^{9} & -e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= (3e^{3})(-e^{-3}) - (-e^{-1})(3e^{9}) \\\\\n&=-3 + 3e^{8} \\\\\n&= 3 (e 8 - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\det(A)\\ne0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that this BVP has the unique trivial solution $y\\equiv0$."] [17592186228888 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y'' = 0, \\qquad y(4) = 0, \\quad y(3) + y'(3) = 0,\\quad 3\\leq x \\leq 4$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A$."] [17592186228888 :question/worked-solution "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n$y''(x) = 0$.\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n$y' = A$.\n\nApplying the condition $y(4) = 0$, we have\n\n$$\n4A + B = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad B = -4A\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny = Ax - 4A = A(x-4)\n$$\n\nApplying the condition\n\n$$\ny(3) + y'(3) = 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n-A+A = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cdot \\left(x-4\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186228421 17592186228422 17592186228423 17592186228424 17592186228426 17592186228427 17592186228428 17592186228429 17592186228430 17592186228432 17592186228433 17592186228434 17592186228436 17592186228437] "immutable_fields" [[17592186228420 :answer-field/id] [17592186228420 :answer-field/key] [17592186228420 :answer-field/type] [17592186228425 :answer-field/id] [17592186228425 :answer-field/key] [17592186228425 :answer-field/type] [17592186228431 :answer-field/id] [17592186228431 :answer-field/key] [17592186228431 :answer-field/type] [17592186228435 :answer-field/id] [17592186228435 :answer-field/key] [17592186228435 :answer-field/type] [17592186228457 :answer-field/id] [17592186228457 :answer-field/key] [17592186228457 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
